---
title: Pineapple Ghost Chile Hot Sauce
description: Ghost chile hot sauce sweetened with pineapple and golden raisins, aged a week in the bottle.
tags: [hot-sauce]
course: condiment
icons: [pineapple, pepper-hot, bottle-droplet]
source:
  name: Food.com
  url: https://www.food.com/recipe/pineapple-ghost-chili-sauce-438373
adapted: true
yield: 4%cups
time: 50m
---

> Gloves are a must when preparing the ghost chiles.

Place the @pineapple{16%oz}(454 g, cubed), @ghost chiles{4}, @onion{1}(small, 6 oz, cubed), @carrot{1}(3 oz, roughly chopped), @golden raisins{1%oz}(28 g, 1/4 cup), @garlic cloves{4}(1 oz), @lime{1}(zest and meat, white pith discarded), @fresh ginger{1/2%oz}(14 g, roughly chopped), @turmeric{1/2%tsp}, @cumin{1/2%tsp}, @cinnamon{1/4%tsp}, @sugar{50%g}(1/4 cup), and @salt{1%tsp} (everything but the vinegar) in a #food processor{} and pulse till pureed.

Add the mixed ingredients to a #pot{} with the @distilled white vinegar{1 1/2%cup}(355 ml, 5% acidity) and cook ~cook{15%minutes}. If needed, blend again for a smooth sauce.

Fill sterilized #hot sauce bottles{}(or jars) with the sauce. Let cool, then cap and refrigerate.

Let age in the fridge for at least 1 week.

> **Storing** — Keeps about a month in the fridge.

> **What's Changed** — The original doesn't say which vinegar. We use 5% distilled white. It also seals the bottles in a 10-minute hot water bath. We skip that and keep the sauce in the fridge.
